  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 216 AM EST Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Anomalous moisture, with PWAT more than 200% of normal, \nand likely near record for the date per Albany, NY sounding \nclimatology, will be present today as a slug of showers slides \nnortheastward across the region this morning. Despite this\nimpressive moisture and very warm air in place, rainfall looks \nmeager as forcing for ascent is weak given a lagging shortwave \ntrough and lack of frontogenesis. That said, low to mid-level \nlapse rates look to be sufficient for some instability and brief\ndownpours in some of the showers, especially towards midday \nwhich helps southern and eastern portions of Vermont have better\nodds of these heavier showers. Think these will be brief such \nthat total rainfall amounts in a given hour will at most be \nabout 0.25\" and mainly up to 0.1\". \n\nAfter this swath of showers and deep moisture exits to our \neast, partial clearing will support some warm season type of \nheating and skinny CAPE profiles that bring us a non-zero chance\nof thunderstorms towards evening. HRRR/RRFS guidance has been \nconsistent in showing a broken line of showers forming in \nnorthern New York and gradually translating eastward with \nincreasing MUCAPE, progged to reach about 200-300 J/kg. Chances \nof thunder in the calibrated REFS data is surprisingly high in \nfar northern Vermont (around 30-40%) based on this CAPE. \nGreatest chances of a rumble of thunder appears to be during the\nmid- afternoon near Route 11 in northern New York and then \nduring the evening in eastern Franklin County in Vermont. Most\nof the remainder of the night, aside from this possible cluster\nof showers exiting to the east, will be dry and mild. Much of\nFriday continues to look dry and unseasonably warm despite being\na bit cooler than today.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: We continue to expect an unsettled weather \npattern for the weekend, as a closed upper level low ambles \neastward from the eastern Great Lakes Friday night into the Gulf\nof Maine by Sunday afternoon. Its slow movement will provide a \nprolonged period of shower chances across the region, but it \nremains hard to pinpoint where/when rain showers will pivot \nacross a particular location. Generally Saturday afternoon \nthrough Saturday night looks like the timeframe where showers \nshould be most abundant over northern New York and Vermont given\nthe proximity of this system, and the track will favor southern\nportions of our region with regards to rainfall amounts. \n\nWhile a big soaking rain is not expected, a wetting rainfall of\n0.25\" through the weekend has increased a bit to a 50-65% \nchance, while rainfall in excess of 1\" is now about 10-15%, \nexcept 20-25% in portions of western Vermont in the Winooski and\nMad River Valleys and the western Adirondack region. With the \nexpected low pressure track, we also should see some increasing \nnortherly flow towards late Saturday but there isn't much cold \nair to advect in. Most guidance shows 850 millibar temperatures \ntoo warm for snow, but at this point have indicated marginally \ncold conditions for some snow to mix in with the rain at our \nhigher elevations in New York before precipitation ends given \nuncertainty in the upper level low track/strength.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Although there remains differences in model solutions, \noverall we look to remain under cyclonic flow for much of next week. \nThe GFS does try to build ridging in briefly for Mon/Mon night, but \nthis is not supported by either the ECMWF or CMC deterministic \nsolutions, which have multiple shortwaves rotating around upper low \npressure centered to our north. Timing of these differ as well, so \nwhile there will likely be some dry days, have stayed with WPC/NBM \nPoPs which give chances for showers through much of the week, \nespecially over the higher terrain. Highs in the 40s/50s mean \nshowers should be rain during the day, but overnight lows near or \nbelow freezing could allow rain to mix with/change over to snow \novernight, especially in the mountains.",
